<section id="A4.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.6 </span>Evaluating Generalization Ability on Diverse Models and Datasets</h3>

<div id="A4.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To validate the generalization ability of our method, we present more experimental results on Qwen-2.5-VL-3B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and different scales of LLaVA series <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>. It can be observed in Table <a href="#A4.T12" title="Table 12 ‣ D.6 Evaluating Generalization Ability on Diverse Models and Datasets ‣ Appendix D Additional Experiments ‣ Hallucination-aware Intermediate Representation Edit in Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> that our method still performs well across different types and scales of LVLMs.</p>
</div>
