</figure>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.7 </span>Method Stability Analysis</h3>

<div id="A4.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the stability of our method, we train the model five times with distinct random seeds and assess its performance on the CHAIR benchmark. As shown in Table <a href="#A4.T15" title="Table 15 ‣ D.7 Method Stability Analysis ‣ Appendix D Additional Experiments ‣ Hallucination-aware Intermediate Representation Edit in Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>, our approach consistently achieves stable results across different runs. On average, our method reduces CHAIR<sub id="A4.SS7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="A4.SS7.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">S</span></sub> and CHAIR<sub id="A4.SS7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_sub"><span id="A4.SS7.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">I</span></sub> by 20.8 and 7.4, respectively, with variances as low as 1.3 and 0.31. These results demonstrate the high stability and reliability of our approach.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.T15" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 15: </span>Evaluation on the stability of HIRE</figcaption>
